<p>This application allows to calculate the apparent reflectivity TOA (<em>Top Of Atmosphere</em>) in remote sensing images captured in the visible spectral region and the apparent temperature of brightness in images of the thermal spectrum.</p>

<p>For the visible spectral region, this model convert an image of values (DN) proportional to the radiance received by the sensor on an image in which the values are reflectances. For the thermal infrared spectral region, image values are transformed into a temperature image expressed in K or in &ordm;C.</p> 
<p>
The apparent reflectivity TOA model implemented for the visible region takes into account the following factors: solar position, the Earth-Sun distance at the time of taking the image and the exoatmospheric solar irradiance (S0) for each channel. In case of the thermal infrared spectral region, the spectral radiance conversion to apparent temperature of brightness is calculated from the calibration constants that are set based on the configuration of the thermal sensor bands.</p>
<p>Currently, the application supports the following sensors:<br>

  <span class="Estilo4">Landsat-1 (MSS)<br>
  Landsat-2 (MSS)<br>
  Landsat-3 (MSS)<br>
  Landsat-4 (MSS)<br>
  Landsat-4 (TM)<br>
  Landsat-5 (MSS)<br>
  Landsat-5 (TM)<br>
  Landsat-7 (ETM+)<br>
  Landsat-8 (OLI)<br>  
  Landsat-8 (OLI-TIRS)<br>    
  SPOT-1 (HRV)<br>
  SPOT-2 (HRV)<br>
  SPOT-5 (HRV)<br>
  SPOT-5 (HRG)<br>  
  SPOT-5 (HRV)<br>
  ASTER (SWIR)<br>    
  ASTER (VIR)<br>  
  CBERS-2 (CCD)<br>    
</span></p>
<p>
To correct images from any other space or airborne sensor, there is an application extension that allows to enter the necessary parameters: solar exoatmospheric irradiance S0 (W/(m&sup2;&middot;sr&middot;&micro;m)) for visible bands and Kl calibration constants (W/(m&sup2;&middot;sr&middot;&micro;m)) and K2 (in K) for thermal infrared bands. For channels of the visible spectrum is also possible to report the atmospheric optical density tau0, parameter used in radiometric correction of images implemented in the Corrad application.</p>


<p>The parameters are saved in a RAD file. It has a section that identifies the version of the program:<br>
  <span class="Estilo4">[VERSIO] <br>
Vers=5</span><br>
a section for reporting the name of the sensor:<br> 
<span class="Estilo4">[SENSOR] <br>
Titol=</span><br>
and the section <span class="Estilo4">[CALIBR]</span> that contains the keys <span class="Estilo4">x_S0</span>, <span class="Estilo4">x_tau0</span>, <span class="Estilo4">x_TempK1</span>, <span class="Estilo4">x_TempK2</span> that identifies the parameters for each channel of <span class="Estilo4">x</span> index. </p>  
<p>
In the case of a multispectral image, the program performs the correction for all the bands in a single run if the Multiband option is checked. In this case, you must specify the output directory and optionally a prefix for the output images.</p>


